╭─ Patch Generation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃     Patch to resolve the issue with in_bulk() on fields in     ┃ │
│ ┃                        UniqueConstraint                        ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue arises because the in_bulk() method in the Manager class │
│ is not implemented to handle fields that are part of a             │
│ UniqueConstraint. The UniqueConstraint class is missing from the   │
│ codebase, which is critical for Django's constraint system. The    │
│ patch adds the UniqueConstraint class and implements the in_bulk() │
│ method to handle fields in UniqueConstraint correctly.             │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│ [file]django/db/models/manager.py[/file] [original] class          │
│ Manager(BaseManager.from_queryset(QuerySet)): pass [/original]     │
│ [patched] class Manager(BaseManager.from_queryset(QuerySet)): def  │
│ in_bulk(self, field_name): """Retrieve a dictionary of model       │
│ instances by field name, even if the field is part of a            │
│ UniqueConstraint.""" if field_name in self._get_field_names(): #   │
│ Check if the field is part of a UniqueConstraint try: constraint = │
│ self._get_unique_constraint(field_name) if constraint: # Field is  │
│ part of a UniqueConstraint, treat it as unique return              │
│ self._get_queryset().in_bulk(field_name)                           │
╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
